WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.160
Are all the peas in a pod the same size?

2
00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:04.960
They can look like a row of twins.

3
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.640
A ruler can help your child look for differences.

4
00:00:11.917 --> 00:00:14.237
Here is the idea your child will find.

5
00:00:15.577 --> 00:00:19.577
Offspring, the young a living thing makes, are like their parents, but not

6
00:00:19.577 --> 00:00:25.417
identical. Along the way, your child practices looking closely, measuring, and

7
00:00:25.417 --> 00:00:28.437
sorting. It takes about thirty minutes.

8
00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:34.290
Set out three or four pea pods from the store, an apple, and a sweet bell pepper.

9
00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:40.670
Add a ruler, paper plate, pencil, cutting board, knife, and animal family pictures.

10
00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:46.230
Set out five or six leaves from one tree or shrub you know is safe to touch.

11
00:00:47.233 --> 00:00:48.753
Stay with your child throughout.

12
00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:51.133
You do all the cutting.

13
00:00:52.533 --> 00:00:55.633
Peas and seeds are choking hazards for children under four.

14
00:00:56.893 --> 00:01:00.473
Keep them out of reach, and call nine one one if a child is choking.

15
00:01:01.733 --> 00:01:03.433
Keep leaves and seeds out of mouths.

16
00:01:04.517 --> 00:01:08.097
Check for pea, peanut, apple, or pepper allergies first.

17
00:01:09.457 --> 00:01:12.857
A child with an allergy can watch while you handle the samples.

18
00:01:14.157 --> 00:01:15.437
Use a sweet bell pepper.

19
00:01:16.717 --> 00:01:20.977
Skip hot peppers. Wash the produce and everyone's hands.

20
00:01:22.217 --> 00:01:24.077
Keep activity samples out of mouths.

21
00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:27.050
Start before you open anything.

22
00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:32.510
Hold up a closed pod and ask, 'Will the peas inside all be the same?'

23
00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:34.230
Write down your child's guess.

24
00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:36.950
You will check it together soon.

25
00:01:37.983 --> 00:01:40.343
Let your child split each pod with their thumbs.

26
00:01:41.703 --> 00:01:45.243
Line up the peas from one pod on the plate, in the order they sat.

27
00:01:46.503 --> 00:01:49.403
Count those peas, then count the peas in another pod.

28
00:01:50.643 --> 00:01:51.943
The numbers often differ.

29
00:01:53.017 --> 00:01:54.597
Now a question for you.

30
00:01:55.617 --> 00:01:56.877
The peas look like twins.

31
00:01:58.237 --> 00:02:01.257
When you lay them next to a ruler, what do you expect to see?

32
00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:08.290
Measure each pea across.

33
00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:13.030
Most garden peas are about one centimeter, or a little smaller.

34
00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:18.550
Your child finds the biggest, the smallest, and any that are paler or flat.

35
00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:21.050
Then go back to the guess.

36
00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:23.890
Ask, 'What did the ruler show?'

37
00:02:24.983 --> 00:02:27.023
All these peas came from one plant.

38
00:02:28.263 --> 00:02:32.263
A bigger pea from this pod had the same parent plant as a smaller one.

39
00:02:33.563 --> 00:02:37.203
Much of the difference comes from how much food reached each pea as it grew.

40
00:02:38.267 --> 00:02:42.407
Cut the apple across its middle to reveal a star of seed pockets.

41
00:02:43.487 --> 00:02:45.367
Cut the pepper from top to bottom.

42
00:02:46.747 --> 00:02:50.327
Line up five seeds from each and compare their sizes and shapes.

43
00:02:51.887 --> 00:02:54.527
Compare the leaves, then measure the longest and shortest.

44
00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:58.900
Show kittens with their mother and ask, 'How are they alike?'

45
00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:01.520
Then show a caterpillar and a butterfly.

46
00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:04.080
Sort the pictures together.

47
00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:10.140
Put babies that look like their parents in one group and babies that look different

48
00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:11.140
at first in another.

49
00:03:12.183 --> 00:03:14.223
Keep the talk to plants and animals.

50
00:03:15.623 --> 00:03:17.843
Do not ask which parent your child looks like.

51
00:03:19.023 --> 00:03:24.183
Many children are adopted, fostered, or live in blended families, and the question

52
00:03:24.183 --> 00:03:25.183
can hurt.

53
00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:29.500
Return to your child's guess and have them show you what they noticed.

54
00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:33.160
Count the peas and seeds and clear the table.

55
00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:36.020
Wash everyone's hands again.

56
00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:41.060
Peas from one pod can be alike in some ways and differ in others.
